{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Phase 1 EXIT — mechanism proven at runtime (rebar-debug)\n\nWorktree `fix-naga-snapshot` @ `origin/main`. One root cause; it explains every symptom the\nticket records. All three handed-in claims re-verified independently — two confirmed, and the\nkey one confirmed by *observation*, not by reading source.\n\n## RC — `materialize_tickets` writes every blob fresh, so adjacent tickets SHAs share nothing\n\n`materialize_tickets` (`src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py:616-724`) content-addresses the\nentry as `<store>/tickets-<sha>` and builds it with `_materialize_tree` (`:373-410`), i.e.\n`git read-tree` into a throwaway index + `git checkout-index --all --force --prefix=<build>/`.\n`checkout-index` materializes the committed blob for **every** tree entry. There is no\nhardlink, no reflink, and no delta against the neighbouring entry that differs by one file.\n\nThe key is the live tickets tip, which advances roughly every 26 s, so the `dest.is_dir()`\ncache-hit branch (`:672`) is effectively never taken.\n\n### E1 — runtime measurement (the ticket's central claim is `dynamic`; source reading would not have sufficed)\n\nScratch store + scratch tracker repo, 300-file tree. Five `materialize_tickets(fetch=False)`\ncalls, each after a commit touching **exactly one** of the 300 files — the shape of a real\ntickets commit (the ticket measured the last 5 consecutive tickets commits at 1 file each).\n\n    res#1 tip=1e150114 -> entries=1 files= 302 bytes= 1.14 MiB\n    res#2 tip=d5d1d23d -> entries=2 files= 602 bytes= 2.29 MiB\n    res#3 tip=eb904552 -> entries=3 files= 902 bytes= 3.43 MiB\n    res#4 tip=48bade46 -> entries=4 files=1202 bytes= 4.58 MiB\n    res#5 tip=934cc0a3 -> entries=5 files=1502 bytes= 5.72 MiB\n\n    RESULT entries_created=5 for 5 resolutions\n           byte_total(accounted)=5.72 MiB   real bytes on disk=5.72 MiB\n           distinct inodes for an IDENTICAL unchanged file across entries: 5 (shared groups=0)\n\nStrictly linear: **5 resolutions -> 5 entries -> 5 trees.** The decisive observation is the\nlast line — `ticket-0100/events.jsonl` is byte-identical in all five commits and still occupies\n**five distinct inodes**. Sharing is not merely rare here; it is structurally absent.\n\nAmplification in this fixture: 300 files rewritten per 1-file delta (300x). On the real store\n(69,984 blobs) the same structure gives ~7x10^4.\n\n**What E1 discriminates against.** It kills \"the key sometimes hits\" (5 entries for 5\nresolutions, zero hits) and \"the janitor would have reclaimed it\" (this measures the\n*production* side in isolation — no janitor ran). It also confirms `itchy-arbored-peccary`'s\naccounting fix is live and correct here: accounted 5.72 MiB == real 5.72 MiB.\n\n### E2 — `SnapshotHandle._cleanup` is a permanent no-op (confirmed, and it is NOT the defect)\n\nHanded in as a claim a sibling agent had already checked; re-verified both ways.\n`grep -rn \"_cleanup\" src/` returns exactly two sites — the field declaration\n(`repo_snapshot.py:199`) and the read in `__exit__` (`:212-213`). **No producer anywhere in\n`src/` assigns it.** Observed at runtime through a real `resolve_gate_handle(...)`:\n\n    handle.source=attested sha=49d6fb2e tickets_path=set\n    handle._cleanup = None\n    after __exit__: cleanup invoked? False  (entry still on disk: True)\n\nSo `__exit__` is inert. **Recorded, but explicitly not the fix**: the entry is a *cache*, and\nper-review release would defeat caching outright rather than repair it. The defect is that the\ncache has no reuse, not that it is never released. Disposition below.\n\n## Field state on this host\n\n64,483 live entries (35,033 `tickets-*`, 29,450 code), consistent with the ticket's\n\"64,000+ / 47.2 GiB\". Nothing was hand-deleted — other sessions are running gates against this\nstore continuously.\n\n## Defect legitimacy — `defect`\n\n`docs/adr/0005-snapshot-cache-architecture.md` D2 grounds the whole layout on \"An immutable SHA\nis a perfect cache key\", and `src/rebar/_snapshot/cache.py:154` restates the operative\nassumption: \"The same SHA is often requested by several concurrent gates.\" For the tickets key\nthat premise is false — E1 shows the same SHA is essentially *never* requested twice. D5\ndescribes a bounded, reclaimable cache; what this key produces is an append-only log of full\ncopies. Behaviour diverges from a cited design invariant, not merely from an expectation.\n\n## Attribution — `swampy-knot-mirage` (615c-7646-4566-4bb8), class `plan_defect`\n\n**Two candidates, and the date test separates them.**\n\n| commit | date | what it did |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ec85e4cd54` | 2026-06-27 | **introduced** `materialize_tickets`: `dest = store / f\"tickets-{sha}\"` + `_materialize_tree`, a full fresh tree per SHA. Trailer `Bug: 615c-7646-4566-4bb8`. |\n| `ae6f2ac57a` | 2026-08-12 | introduced `_pin_tickets_sha`, moving the pin from the code repo's `origin/tickets` mirror to the live tracker `HEAD` (bug 2a6f — the verifier was reading a store 6757 commits stale). |\n\n`ae6f2ac57a` is the tempting answer: it is recent, it sits in the function I am editing, and it\nlands just before the 2026-08-17 saturation window. **It is the wrong answer.** It changed\n*where the pin is read from*, not the copy structure — and on the dominant `fetch=True` path\nthe pre-existing code already called `resolve_ref(\"origin/tickets\", fetch=True)`, which fetches\nand resolves the **live shared tip**. Key velocity was already ~26 s before it. Blaming it would\ncharge a correctness fix for reading a stale store with a write-amplification defect it did not\nintroduce.\n\nThe mechanism has been present unchanged since 2026-06-27 and predates the incident by seven\nweeks; it grew acute only because the tickets tree grew to ~620 MiB. A latent design defect that\nbecame expensive is still attributed to the design.\n\n**Divergence from the blame default, recorded explicitly:** a whole-file `git blame` majority on\n`repo_snapshot.py` would name recent editors, and a blame of the `materialize_tickets` body\nwould surface `ae6f2ac57a`. Neither is the origin. `ec85e4cd54` added a **second populate path**\ninto a store whose content-addressed contract (ADR 0005 D2) was already on the page in front of\nits reviewer, without asking whether the new key domain had the reuse that contract assumes.\nThe consumer is the culprit; ADR 0005's own epic could not have known about a future producer.\n\nThis is the **same origin** `itchy-arbored-peccary` independently attributed for its RC-2, by the\nsame reasoning — arrived at here from a different mechanism, which corroborates rather than\ncopies it. Class `plan_defect`: 615c's plan scoped \"materialize a SEPARATE, read-only, pinned\ncopy of the ticket store\" and the flaw is in that plan, not in a later regression.\n\n## Not absorbed (checked, per the brief)\n\n- `undamaged-epidermic-kakarikis` — janitor only starts in the review-bot process. Untouched:\n  this fix is production-side and reclaims nothing.\n- `famous-digital-dingo` — no mtime bump on a cache hit. Untouched: E1 shows the hit branch is\n  essentially never reached, which is precisely what this fix changes the economics of, but the\n  missing `touch_entry` call is still absent afterwards.\n\nNo description edit — evidence in comments to keep any attestation current."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787293231303595001,"uuid":"4f178711-0139-44c2-a9bd-81702ae56127"}